===Etymology===
From Germanic ''*nauda-'', from Indo-European ''*nau-, *nū-'' ‘death, corpse’.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''[[ned|nēd]]'', Old Saxon ''[[nod|nōd]]'' (Dutch ''[[nood]]''), Old High German ''[[not|nōt]]'' (German ''[[Not]]'' ‘need, hardship, emergency’), Old Norse ''[[nauð]]'' (Swedish ''[[nöd]]''), Gothic {{l|got|𐌽𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃}}.  The Indo-European root is also the source of Lithuanian ''[[novyti|nõvyti]]'' ‘oppress, destroy’, Old Church Slavonic {{l|cu|уныти}} (Russian {{l|ru|ныть}} ‘throbbing pain’).

